Z||000019||skitime||10-26-2001||02:39 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jaguiler:<BR><B>Skitime -  can you please post some pics of the car in your signature?  I want to see the front fascia and the back (hood) too....looks like some really cool driving lights and great looking vents?<BR>Where did you get the center vent for your engine cover?<P>jaguiler</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>All mods except the side scoops I made myself.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7080039-2_2.jpg">   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7080042-1.JPG">   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7080043-1.JPG">   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7080045-1.jpg">   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7080046-1_1.jpg">  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P000821-1.jpg"> <BR>Custom spoiler with driving lights <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P000818-1.JPG">    <BR>Removable rear window <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 10-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||tgowens||10-26-2001||08:45 PM||||The correct color is the "Trim Black" which is a satin finish. They were never painted Gloss Black -- Martin Senour Paints #7244, Plasti-Kote #611, and SEM #39143 are all trim paints.<P>We do see them glossy when they have been shined with Armor All or Vaseline or another substance as a prep for a show. But, to the best of my knowledge they were never painted a gloss black at the factory.<P>I recently looked at a yellow '88 Formula that had only 1 mile on it (it has been in a dealer's showroom since it was received from the factory) and the vents are satin black, not gloss black.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.whgraphics.com" TARGET=_blank>West Highland Graphics & Design</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A><p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 10-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||watts||10-27-2001||12:59 AM||||I think someone else already dropped the name once, but I've had nothing but fantastic results with Mar-Hyde products.<P>The Semi-gloss black looks GREAT on mirrors, the vents, etc.<P>Prep plays a large part in it.  Sand blast them if you can, then wash them down really well with super hot water.  Dry them off, then warm them up to about 80-90F (I personally use a heat gun).  Spray them down in several light coats, waiting about 5 min's between coats until you get the desired 'blackness'.<P>If you REALLY want to get picky after that - and this part gets stinky - fire up your electric (I can't recommend gas!) oven to about 300.  Toss the parts in for 10 min's or so.  Yank out the racks, but DON'T touch the parts for about 1/2 hour - the paint will be soft.  I find it flows it out nicely, and hardens it up.  Gives a great finish - did that to my valve covers (did them in VHT's Bright Red engine enamel) and they look almost like new (better in some ways IMHO).<BR>||||reg||1
